First, find the side length of the equilateral triangle. Since all three sides are equal, divide the perimeter by 3: $159 / 3 = 53$. This value (53) represents the length of one side of the equilateral triangle.

The problem states that one side of the equilateral triangle is the base of the isosceles triangle. Therefore, the length of the base of the isosceles triangle is simply equal to this side length. We do not need to calculate the lengths of the other two legs of the isosceles triangle to answer this specific question, as the base is directly identified.

The base of the isosceles triangle is 53. \boxed{53}

First, we identify the given information directly from the problem statement. Mr. Chiu has 20 silver dollars. The problem explicitly states that Mr. Phung has 53 more silver dollars than Mr. Chiu has. This detail is independent of Mr. Ha's amount or the total sum provided. 

