First, find the train's speed. To cross a signal pole, the train covers its own length of 180 meters in 18 seconds. The speed is $180 \div 18 = 10$ m/s.
Next, determine the distance needed to cross the platform. The train must cover its own length plus the platform length: $180 + 350 = 530$ meters.
Finally, calculate the time required using the formula $\text{Time} = \text{Distance} \div \text{Speed}$. So, $530 \div 10 = 53$ seconds. \boxed{53}

First, find the boat's upstream speed: distance is 75 km in 15 hours, so $75 \div 15 = 5$ km/h. The upstream speed equals the boat's speed in still water ($b$) minus the stream speed ($s=10$). Thus, $b - 10 = 5$, which means $b = 15$ km/h.

Next, calculate the downstream speed: this is $b + s = 15 + 10 = 25$ km/h. The boat travels downstream for 2.12 hours. The distance covered is speed $\times$ time: $25 \times 2.12 = 53$ km.

